          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have stage 3 kidney disease and high blood pressure. Does the classification link the two, and what target does guidance describe for someone like me?",
          "answer": "Your sources do not give a blood pressure target specifically for stage 3 kidney disease, so I can't tell you what number applies to you. Your doctor or kidney team can set that.\n\nThe classification does link the two. In the ICD-10-CM coding system, high blood pressure with chronic kidney disease is coded as I12, with a second code for the kidney disease stage. This is a default rule for recording diagnoses. It is presumed unless the record says otherwise, and it does not require your doctor to have found a cause in your case. The sources I could find did not explain what stage 3 means or how it is measured, so ask your clinician what your stage means for you.\n\nFor treatment, the hypertension guidance says that when chronic kidney disease is present, medicine is usually added alongside lifestyle changes, even at stage 1 blood pressure readings. It also prefers a class of medicines that acts on the renin-angiotensin system, and says kidney function and potassium are checked after starting them. A target of below 130/80 mm Hg appears in guidance for coronary artery disease, but that guidance does not address kidney disease, so it should not be applied to you without checking with your doctor.\n\nGuidance also differs on what counts as high blood pressure. US guidance treats 130/80 mm Hg as the start of hypertension, while European guidance uses 140/90 mm Hg and calls 130 to 139 / 80 to 89 high-normal. The same reading can be labeled differently depending on which guidance is used. The sources did not report the trials behind any kidney-specific target, so I can't say how strong that evidence is.\n\nUseful questions for your clinician: which blood pressure target applies to me, which guideline you are following, and how often my kidney function and potassium will be checked.\n\nIf you ever have a very high reading with chest pain, breathlessness, severe headache, vision loss, confusion, weakness on one side, or trouble speaking, seek emergency care immediately.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
